# SideQuest Mobile - local development env vars
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the real values.
# `.env.local` is gitignored.
#
# Expo loads any EXPO_PUBLIC_* var into process.env at build time
# and embeds it in the JS bundle. Do not put secrets here that
# should stay server-side - only public/anon keys belong here.

# ─── Backend API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEFAULT — the deployed backend. This is what the normal Expo Go workflow
# uses, and the same API every EAS build profile ships with (see eas.json), so
# the phone behaves the same in Expo Go as in the App Store build. No local
# backend needs to be running.
#
# The app logs the resolved base URL, host and port on startup (dev builds
# only), so you can confirm at a glance which API it is actually calling.
#
# ── OPTIONAL: run against a backend on THIS machine ───────────────────────
# Only needed when you are actively changing backend code. Not the default —
# leaving it set means Expo Go stops working the moment that backend is not
# running, which looks exactly like the app being broken.
#
#   1. Find this computer's LAN IP (phone and computer on the same Wi-Fi):
#        Windows:  ipconfig                  → "IPv4 Address" (e.g. 192.168.0.10)
#        macOS:    ipconfig getifaddr en0
#      It is DHCP-assigned and changes when you reconnect.
#
#   2. Swap the value below for that IP plus the backend's HTTP port (5079 —
#      see backend/Properties/launchSettings.json):
#        EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.0.10:5079
#
#      No trailing slash, no path — the app appends "/api/..." itself.
#      NEVER localhost / 127.0.0.1 here: on a phone those mean the phone.
#      NEVER 10.0.2.2 either — that only resolves inside the Android emulator.
#
#   3. The backend must listen on 0.0.0.0, not localhost. The "http" profile in
#      launchSettings.json already does (http://0.0.0.0:5079).
#
#   4. Allow the port through the computer's firewall, or the phone's requests
#      are dropped silently and every call just times out. On Windows, once,
#      from an elevated PowerShell:
#        New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName "SideQuest backend 5079" `
#          -Direction Inbound -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 5079 `
#          -Profile Private -Action Allow
#
#   5. Verify with http://<LAN-IP>:5079/health from the phone's browser — it
#      needs no auth, no database and no storage, so a reply there means the
#      transport is fine and anything still failing is above it.
#
#   6. Put this back to https://api.sidequesttravel.app when you are done.
#
# Editing this file requires a FULL Metro restart either way: EXPO_PUBLIC_*
# values are resolved into Expo's virtual env module when the dev server
# starts, so a running Metro keeps serving the old value.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.sidequesttravel.app

# ─── Website base URL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used as a fallback for password-reset email redirects in Expo Go.
# In native builds, deep links (sidequest://) are used instead.
EXPO_PUBLIC_WEB_URL=https://sidequesttravel.app

# ─── Supabase (client-side) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Anon/publishable key only - safe in client code.
# Never put service_role keys here.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=YOUR_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY

# ─── Optional dev flags ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to "1" to disable the developer auto-login shortcut in dev mode.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_DISABLE_DEV_AUTO_LOGIN=1
